WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:03.960
Imagine charging a capacitor.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.220
Current flows in the wires, but no conduction charge crosses an ideal vacuum

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:10.360
gap between the plates.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.880
Does the magnetic field description simply break at the gap?

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.820
Maxwell's correction makes the account consistent.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.780
A changing electric flux contributes to the magnetic circulation just as

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:22.700
conduction current does.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:27.959
In vacuum, displacement current equals epsilon zero times the rate of change

00:00:27.959 --> 00:00:28.080
of

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:28.760
electric flux.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:33.920
Its units are amperes, but it is not electrons flying across the vacuum gap.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.020
For an ideal parallel plate vacuum capacitor, neglecting fringing, electric

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:42.600
flux through the full plate area is charge divided by epsilon zero.

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.660
Differentiate and displacement current equals the rate of change of plate

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:47.000
charge.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.460
That matches the wire's charging current.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:54.680
Since charge equals C V for fixed capacitance, displacement current is C times

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:55.660
the voltage derivative.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.224
Take capacitance two nanofarads and voltage increasing at five hundred

00:01:00.224 --> 00:01:00.640
thousand

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:01.420
volts per second.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.620
Convert capacitance to two times ten to the minus nine farad.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:09.940
The current is zero point zero zero one ampere, or one milliampere.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.886
A constant capacitor voltage gives zero displacement current in this model

00:01:14.886 --> 00:01:15.120
even

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.920
though the electric field can be nonzero.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:20.440
The electric flux formula stated here uses vacuum.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.400
In matter, the general description uses electric displacement and must include

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:26.440
the material response.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.520
Do not use the vacuum expression as a universal dielectric shortcut.

00:01:30.768 --> 00:01:34.128
Faraday's law connects changing magnetic fields to electric fields.

00:01:35.048 --> 00:01:37.728
Maxwell's correction completes the changing electric field side.

00:01:38.668 --> 00:01:42.168
Together, the equations allow electromagnetic disturbances to travel even

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:42.708
through vacuum.

00:01:43.448 --> 00:01:46.188
We do not need air or a vibrating material string.

00:01:47.208 --> 00:01:49.688
Accelerated charges can produce electromagnetic radiation.

00:01:49.828 --> 00:01:54.148
For a plane wave travelling in vacuum, the electric and magnetic fields are

00:01:54.148 --> 00:01:57.588
perpendicular to one another, and both are perpendicular to the propagation

00:01:57.588 --> 00:01:58.128
direction.

00:01:58.128 --> 00:02:00.168
That makes the wave transverse.

00:02:00.928 --> 00:02:04.668
At a given point, the electric and magnetic oscillations are in phase in this

00:02:04.668 --> 00:02:05.508
travelling wave model.

00:02:06.128 --> 00:02:08.548
They reach corresponding zeroes and extrema together.

00:02:08.548 --> 00:02:14.248
Their amplitudes are not numerically equal in SI units: E zero equals c times

00:02:14.248 --> 00:02:15.548
B zero.

00:02:15.548 --> 00:02:18.048
Suppose electric field amplitude is ninety volts per meter.

00:02:18.848 --> 00:02:22.848
With c approximately three times ten to the eight meters per second, magnetic

00:02:22.848 --> 00:02:27.348
amplitude is three times ten to the minus seven tesla, or zero point three

00:02:27.348 --> 00:02:27.968
microtesla.

00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:29.788
Now use a direction check.

00:02:30.328 --> 00:02:35.248
If E points along positive y and B along positive z at an instant, E cross B

00:02:35.248 --> 00:02:36.528
points along positive x.

00:02:37.048 --> 00:02:39.328
That is the energy flow and propagation direction.

00:02:39.968 --> 00:02:44.088
At the opposite half cycle, both fields reverse, so their cross product still

00:02:44.088 --> 00:02:44.668
points forward.

00:02:45.148 --> 00:02:49.188
The sketch shows directions at one location; it is not a picture of a single

00:02:49.188 --> 00:02:51.808
charge moving through space along every drawn field arrow.

00:02:52.128 --> 00:02:56.368
Frequency counts oscillations per second, and wavelength is the spatial repeat

00:02:56.368 --> 00:02:56.768
distance.

00:02:57.428 --> 00:03:01.268
For any travelling periodic wave, speed equals frequency times wavelength.

00:03:02.068 --> 00:03:06.608
In vacuum, the electromagnetic wave speed is c, approximately three hundred

00:03:06.608 --> 00:03:07.788
million meters per second.

00:03:08.588 --> 00:03:12.628
Radio waves and visible light share that vacuum speed; higher frequency does

00:03:12.628 --> 00:03:14.328
not mean faster travel in vacuum.

00:03:14.708 --> 00:03:18.208
Let's calculate for frequency six times ten to the fourteen hertz.

00:03:18.888 --> 00:03:23.528
Divide c by frequency to get five times ten to the minus seven meter, or five

00:03:23.528 --> 00:03:24.208
hundred nanometers.

00:03:25.208 --> 00:03:26.628
Take a moment with the powers of ten.

00:03:27.248 --> 00:03:31.078
One nanometer is ten to the minus nine meter, so the conversion is five

00:03:31.078 --> 00:03:32.028
hundred, not five.

00:03:32.768 --> 00:03:36.868
Now let this wave enter a stationary transparent medium with refractive index

00:03:36.868 --> 00:03:38.708
one point five at that frequency.

00:03:39.328 --> 00:03:43.248
Its phase speed is c over n, or two hundred million meters per second.

00:03:43.708 --> 00:03:47.648
The source and interface keep the frequency at six times ten to the fourteen

00:03:47.648 --> 00:03:47.968
hertz.

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:51.788
Wavelength becomes about three hundred thirty three nanometers in the medium.

00:03:52.568 --> 00:03:55.208
Do not change the frequency just because the speed changed.

00:03:56.028 --> 00:04:00.008
Spectrum classifications ordinarily quote vacuum wavelength; the shorter

00:04:00.008 --> 00:04:03.228
wavelength inside glass does not turn the light into a different frequency

00:04:03.228 --> 00:04:03.548
band.

00:04:04.388 --> 00:04:08.268
Real media can be dispersive, so refractive index can depend on frequency.

00:04:08.268 --> 00:04:11.588
We use the stated index at this particular frequency.

00:04:11.928 --> 00:04:16.208
The electromagnetic spectrum is one continuous family, not seven unrelated

00:04:16.208 --> 00:04:16.948
kinds of wave.

00:04:17.828 --> 00:04:21.448
Starting from lower frequency and moving upward, the usual teaching order is

00:04:21.448 --> 00:04:26.308
radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X rays and gamma rays.

00:04:27.148 --> 00:04:31.088
Vacuum wavelength runs in the opposite direction because c equals frequency

00:04:31.088 --> 00:04:31.868
times wavelength.

00:04:32.648 --> 00:04:37.008
The board uses equal width named bands only as an ordering guide, not a linear

00:04:37.008 --> 00:04:37.788
frequency scale.

00:04:38.448 --> 00:04:42.628
Microwaves are often treated as a subset of radio waves, so the labels are not

00:04:42.628 --> 00:04:43.988
perfectly exclusive boxes.

00:04:44.948 --> 00:04:46.588
Visible light is a small part of the spectrum.

00:04:47.368 --> 00:04:50.608
A useful approximate classroom range is four hundred to seven hundred

00:04:50.608 --> 00:04:54.588
nanometers in vacuum, with actual visibility limits depending on conditions.

00:04:55.408 --> 00:04:58.868
Red is toward the longer wavelength end; violet toward the shorter wavelength

00:04:58.868 --> 00:04:59.228
end.

00:04:59.988 --> 00:05:03.588
Infrared lies beyond the red side and ultraviolet beyond the violet side.

00:05:04.288 --> 00:05:07.428
Suppose wave A has frequency twice that of wave B in vacuum.

00:05:08.168 --> 00:05:09.028
What happens to wavelength?

00:05:09.568 --> 00:05:12.168
It halves, while their vacuum speeds remain the same.

00:05:13.128 --> 00:05:16.708
Exact boundaries between bands are conventions, not abrupt changes in the laws

00:05:16.708 --> 00:05:17.188
of physics.

00:05:17.768 --> 00:05:21.768
In particular, X ray and gamma ray frequency ranges can overlap; their

00:05:21.768 --> 00:05:23.988
production mechanisms can help distinguish the names.

00:05:24.688 --> 00:05:28.048
Learn the order, the inverse wavelength trend and the shared wave properties.

00:05:28.688 --> 00:05:32.508
Later, photon energy calculations will give another way to compare frequencies

00:05:32.508 --> 00:05:34.288
without changing this spectrum picture.

00:05:35.328 --> 00:05:39.648
Applications become easier to remember when you connect them to interactions

00:05:40.148 --> 00:05:41.448
rather than learning a random list.

00:05:42.248 --> 00:05:44.228
Radio waves carry communication signals.

00:05:45.288 --> 00:05:49.168
Microwaves are useful for radar and many communication links; suitable

00:05:49.168 --> 00:05:52.648
microwave fields can also transfer energy to food through absorption.

00:05:53.568 --> 00:05:57.788
These examples do not mean every material absorbs every microwave frequency

00:05:57.788 --> 00:05:58.228
equally.

00:05:59.108 --> 00:06:01.828
Infrared is important in thermal sensing and remote controls.

00:06:02.538 --> 00:06:07.068
Thermal radiation is not exclusively infrared; its distribution depends on

00:06:07.068 --> 00:06:07.468
temperature.

00:06:08.488 --> 00:06:11.448
Visible light supports illumination, photography and vision.

00:06:12.398 --> 00:06:16.288
Ultraviolet has controlled applications such as germicidal equipment and

00:06:16.288 --> 00:06:17.328
fluorescence excitation.

00:06:18.428 --> 00:06:21.668
X rays support imaging because different materials attenuate them differently.

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:26.508
Gamma rays are associated with energetic nuclear processes and have controlled

00:06:26.508 --> 00:06:28.468
scientific and industrial applications.

00:06:29.388 --> 00:06:34.138
Penetration and biological effects depend on photon energy and material; do

00:06:34.138 --> 00:06:34.328
not

00:06:34.328 --> 00:06:36.108
infer safety from a band name alone.

00:06:36.988 --> 00:06:40.988
Our lesson is a physics overview, not instructions for operating radiation

00:06:40.988 --> 00:06:41.428
equipment.

00:06:42.568 --> 00:06:44.048
Let's finish with two calculations.

00:06:44.828 --> 00:06:49.008
A three gigahertz signal has frequency three times ten to the nine hertz, so

00:06:49.008 --> 00:06:53.008
vacuum wavelength is zero point one meter, ten centimeters.

00:06:53.008 --> 00:06:57.168
A wave at three times ten to the fourteen hertz has wavelength one micrometer,

00:06:57.168 --> 00:06:58.628
in the infrared region.

00:06:59.588 --> 00:07:01.388
Both still travel at c in vacuum.

00:07:02.268 --> 00:07:06.288
Your checklist is electric flux change, field directions, the propagation

00:07:06.288 --> 00:07:10.808
medium, frequency wavelength conversion and the interaction relevant to the

00:07:10.808 --> 00:07:11.228
application.

00:07:12.148 --> 00:07:14.688
Those links make the spectrum a useful physical map.
